abstract: >-
  'Geochronological data are used across many different
  fields of geology. As analyses become cheaper and easier
  and online databases grow, researchers have access to an
  abundance of data for large-scale studies. However,
  datasets reported from different labs, researchers, and
  institutions all have their own formatting, and community
  reporting standards continue to evolve. Existing databases
  and compilations are incomplete and may not have all the
  metadata a researcher wants. Powerful analysis tools also
  require a variety of input formats. The current common
  workflow of spreadsheet manipulation is time-consuming and
  tedious. We present GeoCORK, an open-source desktop
  application to address this problem by importing and
  storing U-Pb geochronology data in a personal, local SQL
  database. The researcher interacts with the database
  through user-friendly interfaces which require no coding
  experience to import, organize, filter, and export U-Pb
  analyses and metadata. Data can be imported and exported
  in preset or custom formats and filtered into groups based
  on custom criteria. Tag categories are intentionally broad
  to allow for personalized metadata. We also present two
  example zircon U-Pb databases: a small compilation from
  the Klamath Mountains (5,764 analyses) with several
  different data types and formats and a large global
  compilation (>1.8 million analyses) with more uniform
  formatting. GeoCORK allows researchers to make the most of
  available U-Pb data, and it is designed to incorporate
  other geologic methods in the future.'
keywords:
  - geochronology
  - uranium lead dating
  - research data management
  - geochemistry
